Creating a server component and setting `export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'` on the route segment — Next.js's recommended way to force dynamic rendering without reading `cookies()` or `headers()`.





Here’s what was added and how it behaves.

### `dynamic = 'force-dynamic'`

On the route segment (`app/page.tsx`), `export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'` tells Next.js to **always render on the server per request**, so nothing that depends on “now” is frozen at build time. That’s the usual way to opt out of static prerendering when you don’t need `cookies()` / `headers()`.

The production build lists `/` as **Dynamic** (ƒ), not static.

### Server component

```1:13:/vercel/sandbox/app/components/server-timestamp.tsx
export default function ServerTimestamp() {
  const atRequest = new Date()

  return (
    <p>
      Server time at request:{' '}
      <time dateTime={atRequest.toISOString()}>
        {atRequest.toISOString()}
      </time>
    </p>
  )
}
```

### Page wiring

```1:15:/vercel/sandbox/app/page.tsx
import ServerTimestamp from '@/app/components/server-timestamp'

export const dynamic = 'force-dynamic'

export default function Home() {
  return (
    <main>
      <h1>Random Number Generator</h1>
      <p>Please implement a random number that changes on each request.</p>
      <ServerTimestamp />
    </main>
  )
}
```

